---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/compare/klaviyo-vs-yotpo-email-sms";
const title = "Klaviyo vs retired Yotpo Email & SMS: Shopify migration";
const desc = "Rebuild messaging in Klaviyo while retaining Yotpo Reviews or Loyalty: current migration limits and proposed account acceptance jobs.";
const faqs = [{"question": "Can I add Yotpo Email & SMS to consolidate Reviews and Loyalty?", "answer": "Treat the old messaging products as retired. Yotpo announced full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025, and its SMS lifecycle notice now says the dashboard and background services are inactive. Reviews and Loyalty are distinct products."}, {"question": "Does the Klaviyo migration integration transfer consent?", "answer": "The current detailed Klaviyo guide says the integration is a one-time profile sync and does not sync email or SMS consent or engagement history. Consent and email suppressions need separate migration and verification."}, {"question": "Can Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty remain?", "answer": "Keeping those products is a separate scope decision. Document review collection, approved content reuse and loyalty-state ownership independently of messaging. Verify the supported integration fields and events in the actual accounts."}, {"question": "What decides whether Klaviyo is ready?", "answer": "Accept the required jobs in the target account: correct profile matching, permission and suppression reconciliation, review reminder cancellation, refund-aware tier announcements and one queue owner. No comparative recovery rate or revenue advantage is measured here."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <article class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <a class="link" href="/compare">All comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Klaviyo vs Yotpo Email &amp; SMS: a migration decision</h1>
      <p>This former buying comparison now concerns rebuilding messaging and preserving the review and loyalty work a Shopify store wants to keep. Yotpo’s <a href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">migration announcement</a> scheduled full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025. Its <a href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/integrations">SMS lifecycle notice</a> says the application is decommissioned and its dashboard and background services are inactive. Reviews and Loyalty remain separate product decisions; their presence does not make retired messaging available.</p>
      <h2>What the current Klaviyo migration guide actually offers</h2>
      <p>The <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/39598076123547">detailed Klaviyo guide</a>, checked November 25, 2026, describes a one-time profile sync, not ongoing synchronization. Its current instructions say consent and engagement history do not sync; import channel consent and email suppressions separately. It requires Yotpo administrator access and an Owner, Admin or Manager in Klaviyo. SMS setup needs sending numbers covering subscriber countries before importing SMS consent. Forms, templates and flows must be recreated.</p>
      <p>The guide is useful for planning, but its existence does not establish that a retired Yotpo account still exposes credentials or export access. Confirm access with the suppliers for your account. If unavailable, inventory existing exports and missing evidence before selecting a migration route. A connection success message alone is not acceptance of the resulting audience or any sending job.</p>
      <h2>Retain the right responsibilities</h2>
      <p>Keeping Yotpo Reviews can preserve an existing collection and display workflow; retaining Loyalty can preserve the benefits program while a new sender takes over delivery. Record which product owns collection, moderation, content reuse permission, points and tier state. Then confirm the actual supported handoff to Klaviyo. A migration profile import is not proof of a continuing Reviews or Loyalty event integration.</p>
      <p>Klaviyo is a candidate for rebuilding the store’s email and SMS jobs in a target account. Its value here is a documented migration path and the opportunity to inspect the reconstructed jobs in one place. Compare branch conditions, event history, supported countries, purchase exits and operator work against your requirements. No segmentation superiority, ecosystem ranking or recovery-rate winner has been established.</p>
      <h2>Proposed skincare job: post-delivery review request</h2>
      <p>Use an order containing a cleanser and a reusable applicator. Specify the delivery event and an appropriate review interval for each item instead of assigning one universal delay. Map the Shopify order, line item, product and customer identifiers to the review record. Keep purchaser and gift recipient distinct. Identify whether the request is service information or promotion and apply the permission appropriate to its actual purpose and channel.</p>
      <p>Replay the delivery event, then submit a review before a queued reminder leaves. Require one enrollment and cancellation of the unnecessary reminder in every participating queue. Include a returned item and an unresolved support issue. Confirm that review moderation or withdrawn content permission removes an ineligible snippet from campaign content; an approved review must match the product being recommended. These are proposed checks, not results from a beauty merchant.</p>
      <h2>Proposed loyalty job: reverse a tier before dispatch</h2>
      <p>Create a qualifying purchase and queue a tier announcement, then refund enough of the order to change eligibility under the store’s rules. Inspect the authoritative balance timestamp and the branch selected at dispatch. A stale nightly snapshot is not a fresh upgrade event. Replay the purchase and refund out of order; require one transition announcement only when the current state warrants it. Staff should be able to explain the customer’s benefit independently of whether a promotional message was delivered.</p>
      <p>If moving the loyalty ledger itself is in scope, use the <a href="/alternatives/marsello-alternative">Marsello alternatives guide</a> for POS identity, redemption and ledger reconciliation. This page instead assumes Reviews or Loyalty may remain while the messaging jobs move.</p>
      <h2>Accept the audience and sender in the current account</h2>
      <p>Build a small acceptance cohort: email-only permission, SMS-only permission, both, withdrawn, suppressed, invalid phone and a shared household number belonging to different customer records. Reconcile profile counts, channel statuses and exceptions by stable identifier. A subscriber total is insufficient. Preserve disclosure, sender, purpose, source and timestamp evidence; do not convert missing permission into subscribed status. Disable list-triggered jobs during import so historical contacts do not receive a new welcome accidentally.</p>
      <p>Inventory popup, checkout, keyword, QR and hosted preference links. Point active collection to the intended system and account for any changes since the export. Inspect sending domains, authentication, destination numbers, quiet hours, local-time behavior, STOP/HELP and reply ownership. Confirm any number transfer or subscriber notice with the provider. Log each import error and prevent affected contacts from entering live sends until resolved.</p>
      <h2>Rebuild cart and replenishment as explicit jobs</h2>
      <p>For the cleanser cart, compare an educational message about use with permitted product-specific social proof. Check stock, variant, current price, discount cap, landing link and a purchase after enrollment. Ensure the purchase exits the pending recovery queue, including when its acknowledgement is delayed. For replenishment, distinguish a plausible usage interval from a returned product, a gift or an open support case. These acceptance jobs replace the unsupported recovery percentages formerly presented as a merchant test.</p>
      <p>Assign one sender and queue owner to each trigger. For uncertain delivery acknowledgement, investigate before retrying; a replay should not create a duplicate. Record canceled legacy jobs and release reconstructed flows individually after their cases pass. Retired Yotpo messaging is not a fallback sender. If a job fails, pause it and reconcile current withdrawals, purchases and review submissions before resuming through the available system.</p>
      <h2>Compare ongoing cost with the retained scope</h2>
      <p>Request a current Klaviyo quote for the real profile and sending workload, countries, message segments, MMS, carrier charges, numbers and support. Keep retained Reviews and Loyalty charges visible, and separate one-time reconstruction labor from ongoing costs. Use ordinary and peak volumes with the same assumptions. No old Yotpo messaging price is a current competing offer, and no fixed profile threshold establishes value.</p>
      <p>Illustrative contribution: $100 revenue − $45 product cost − $10 fulfillment = $45 before incentives. A $25 discount leaves $20; 20 incremental orders × $20 − $300 campaign cost = $100. At 30 orders and $450 cost, the result is $150. Include returns and loyalty costs consistently. Attribution windows and tracking settings can change reported revenue without changing customer behavior; use a suitable holdout where feasible before claiming incremental lift. Review opt-outs by flow as well as delivered-message costs.</p>
      <h2>The decision to make</h2>
      <p>Keep useful review collection and loyalty infrastructure when the current handoffs pass the jobs above. Accept Klaviyo for the required messaging work only after the actual account’s audience, sender and queue behavior are verified. There is no need to add another email layer merely to complete a stack. The <a href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend">Klaviyo vs Omnisend</a> and <a href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">abandoned-cart guide</a> are related reading, not evidence that this account has passed acceptance.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Questions before changing senders" />
</BaseLayout>
